/* Wealth Lab · Module 12 Retirement and Tax-Advantaged Accounts · 2026-11-02 */
window.WealthLabAcademicM12Data={"schema":"wealth-lab-m12-academic-v1","version":"3.0.0","reviewDate":"2026-11-02","taxYear":2026,"lessonIds":["L12.S1","L12.S2","L12.S3","L12.S4"],"lessons":[{"id":"L12.S1","title":"401(k), Employer Match and Vesting","subtitle":"Workplace retirement design, 2026 contribution law, ownership, employer incentives and portfolio governance","prerequisites":["L1.S2 household cash-flow and obligation tracking","L7.S3 time value of money and present value","L10.S4 investment policy statements","L11.S1 wage income and tax reporting"],"objectives":["Map an employee's paycheck, plan deferral, match, fees and ownership rights","Calculate match formulas, contribution thresholds, per-paycheck amounts and true-up risk","Apply 2026 employee deferral, catch-up and annual-addition rules to a clearly described plan","Distinguish immediate employee ownership from vesting of certain employer contributions","Explain how traditional and Roth salary deferrals affect cash flow and current federal tax","Audit an actual summary plan description, annual fee notice and investment menu","Identify the liquidity and default risks of loans, hardship withdrawals and job transitions"],"sections":[{"title":"A 401(k) is an employer-sponsored legal account, not an investment by itself","body":["A workplace retirement plan is a tax-governed container. The owner selects investments permitted by the plan, while trustees and administrators maintain account records and employer rules. A 401(k) may hold diversified index funds, target-date funds, company stock or insurance-like products; risk depends on underlying assets, not on the label 401(k). Eligibility, enrollment, employer contributions, vesting, distributions and expenses are governed by plan documents and federal law. A worker must read the summary plan description rather than assume every workplace offers identical terms.","Follow the money from paycheck to retirement account. An employee elects a salary deferral, payroll deducts it, the employer remits it and any match or profit-sharing contribution, and the custodian buys investments. Traditional salary deferrals generally reduce current W-2 Box 1 wages but do not eliminate Social Security and Medicare payroll tax. Designated Roth deferrals are made with after-tax income for federal income-tax purposes. Employee contributions are part of compensation directed to savings; employer matches are additional compensation subject to eligibility and plan terms.","A practical account statement must distinguish gross balance, vested balance, Roth versus pretax source, loan principal, investments and current cash needs. Gross account value is not equivalent to an emergency fund: distributions can face restrictions, taxes and penalties. A future home purchase cannot count the same dollar as both down-payment liquidity and safely invested retirement capital."],"worked":["Gross compensation $60,000 with 6% elective deferral gives $3,600 annual employee investment.","If all 24 paychecks have equal gross pay, deferral is $150 per paycheck.","A hypothetical employer match of $1,800 is additional to the employee's $3,600.","Gross balance includes amounts that may not be vested or withdrawable. Mark restricted funds separately."],"practice":[["Does a 401(k) guarantee a fixed return?","No. It is a tax-advantaged employer account structure, and returns depend on available investments, fees and the plan's risks. A target-date name is not a guarantee."],["Why is a gross plan balance an unsafe house down-payment number?","Some funds may be unvested, invested in volatile assets, subject to withdrawal limits or future taxation; actual liquidity must be verified."]]},{"title":"Dissect the match formula: the free-money slogan misses constraints","body":["Employer matches use a specified fraction of qualifying compensation or employee contributions. The phrase '50% of the first 6% you contribute' means that if you defer 6% of eligible pay, the employer contributes an extra 3% of pay, not 50% of your entire salary. An employer offering '100% of the first 3%' provides a 3% of pay match when the employee contributes at least 3%. Different compensation definitions, eligible pay caps and excluded bonuses can alter the calculation.","With a hypothetical $60,000 salary and 50% match on the first 6% of eligible pay, an employee contribution of $3,600 produces a $1,800 employer match. At 3% employee deferral, employee $1,800 and employer $900; at 10%, employee $6,000 but the match still caps at $1,800. A match can be highly valuable when vested, yet its true value must consider cash-flow pressure, fees, plan risks and the possibility of early departure.","Cash available matters. If rent, utilities and emergency savings are underfunded, racing to a match may force costly credit-card borrowing. Analyze monthly surplus and expensive debt before recommending a percentage. The correct plan often balances enough match capture with protected bills, high-interest debt reduction and immediate household liquidity."],"worked":["Salary $60,000; employee 6% = $3,600.","50% employer match on first 6% ⇒ 0.5×$3,600 = $1,800.","Employee 10% = $6,000; employer match remains $1,800 under the example's cap.","Total annual retirement additions from these two sources = $7,800 before earnings, fees and vesting."],"practice":[["Does contributing 10% to this plan generate a $3,000 employer match?","No. The hypothetical plan only matches 50% of the first 6% deferred; the maximum annual match at $60,000 eligible pay is $1,800."],["Is maximizing the employer match appropriate even when doing so causes 29% credit-card interest?","Not necessarily. Model the true household funding consequences and vesting. A high borrowing cost and unpaid essentials can offset the apparent match value."]]},{"title":"Per-paycheck match, true-ups and bonus timing","body":["Some plans compute the match separately for each pay period. A worker who contributes the annual salary-deferral maximum by September may stop deferring for the final months and lose payroll-specific matching contributions in October through December, even when their annual contribution percentage was high. Other plans have an annual true-up that restores the match to the full-year formula. The summary plan description, payroll record and employer's true-up policy establish which outcome applies.","Suppose a worker earns $60,000 in 24 equal paychecks ($2,500 each), with 50% match on deferrals up to 6% of each paycheck. To capture the full per-paycheck match, they must defer at least $150 each payroll, producing $75 employer match each period, or $1,800 for the year. If eligible compensation includes bonuses, rerun the computation. A front-loaded elective deferral can create a missed match unless the plan has a restoring true-up.","Employer contribution deadlines may differ from each payroll deposit, and a plan may impose eligibility or last-day-of-year requirements for certain employer contributions. Statement timing alone does not prove the benefit was denied; reconcile the final plan year. A professional worksheet specifies whether employer matching is per period, annual, discretionary or subject to additional conditions."],"worked":["24 checks × $2,500 eligible wages = $60,000 annual compensation.","6% per-paycheck employee amount = $150; 50% match = $75.","24×$75 = $1,800 full-year employer match with continuous qualifying deferrals.","If qualifying deferrals occur for only 18 paychecks and no true-up, 18×$75=$1,350 match; $450 of potential match was missed."],"practice":[["Why can contributing the full annual deferral limit early reduce an employer match?","The plan may only match periods with employee deferrals. If contributions stop and no annual true-up exists, late payroll periods can earn no match."],["Which document should the employee inspect before front-loading contributions?","The plan's actual match computation and annual true-up terms in the summary plan description and related employer communications."]]},{"title":"2026 statutory limits: distinguish employee deferrals from total annual additions","body":["For 2026, the usual 401(k), 403(b), governmental 457(b) and federal Thrift Savings Plan elective deferral limit is $24,500, subject to relevant special coordination and plan rules. The normal 401(k) catch-up for an eligible participant age 50 or older is $8,000, for a combined $32,500, if the plan permits it. Under SECURE 2.0, workers turning 60 through 63 during 2026 have a higher catch-up limit of $11,250, yielding $35,750 for eligible plans. These are not IRA limits and do not imply every worker can contribute that much regardless of earnings.","The separate 2026 defined contribution annual additions limit is generally $72,000 before qualifying catch-up contributions, subject also to compensation and plan rules. It aggregates employee elective deferrals, most employer matches, nonelective contributions and forfeiture allocations. Catch-up amounts are generally outside the regular annual-additions ceiling. For 2026, certain higher-earning workers whose prior-year employer wages exceeded $150,000 must make applicable catch-up contributions as Roth contributions in plans subject to the new rule; this does not make all elective deferrals Roth.","Multiple jobs complicate the math. An employee changing employers may need to aggregate elective deferrals across unrelated 401(k)-type plans; payroll systems cannot necessarily see each other. Annual limit violations can require timely corrections. The correct question is not 'what is the maximum?' but 'what may this particular worker contribute under tax law and their current plans?'"],"worked":["Age 42 and plan permitting: ordinary 2026 elective deferral ceiling $24,500.","Age 52 with eligible catch-up: $24,500+$8,000=$32,500.","Age 61 with higher catch-up: $24,500+$11,250=$35,750.","Employee $20,000 plus employer $12,000 generally contributes $32,000 toward the $72,000 non-catch-up annual-additions ceiling, subject to other sources."],"practice":[["Can the employer match be added to the $24,500 elective deferral to determine employee elective deferral limit?","No. The employee elective-deferral ceiling and total annual-additions ceiling are distinct. Employer additions usually count toward the latter, not the former."],["Does age 60 automatically guarantee an $11,250 2026 catch-up?","No. The employee must satisfy eligibility, earned-compensation and relevant plan rules, and the plan must permit the applicable catch-up contributions."]]},{"title":"Vesting converts a conditional employer promise into owned retirement property","body":["Vesting describes legally owned plan benefits. An employee's elective deferrals and related earnings are always fully vested. Certain employer matches and profit-sharing contributions can vest over time under a plan schedule. Two common permitted structures are a three-year cliff (zero until three years of vesting service, then 100%) or a six-year graded pattern (20% after year two, 40% year three, 60% year four, 80% year five, 100% year six). Safe-harbor designs and other plan types may require faster or immediate vesting.","Suppose the plan displays $12,000 from the employee and $8,000 from employer contributions, ignoring earnings for clarity. At 40% employer vesting, the worker owns $12,000+$3,200=$15,200, not the headline $20,000. If the worker resigns, $4,800 may be forfeited under the plan's actual rules. Vesting service is not always identical to years printed on the employee's résumé: plan definitions, breaks in service and previous employer service recognition can matter.","Job decisions should compare compensation and career benefits as well as the value of unvested money. Waiting another year solely to vest is not necessarily optimal if a new job offers more wages, better insurance or much better work conditions. The financially literate employee asks for the official vesting percentage and effective dates, then computes the opportunity cost of leaving or staying."],"worked":["Employee source balance $12,000 is fully vested.","Employer source $8,000 at 40% vesting ⇒ $3,200 owned.","Total owned/vested $15,200; unvested employer balance $4,800.","Compare the potential forfeiture with new-job compensation and plan benefits before making an employment decision."],"practice":[["Does a company have the right to reclaim an employee's own elective deferrals when they resign?","No. Employee elective deferrals are always 100% vested; only certain employer-provided contributions may remain subject to vesting."],["Can a person with three years employed assume they are always fully vested?","No. Vesting depends on plan design and credited vesting service. A six-year graded plan might vest 40% at three credited years; other plans can be faster."]]},{"title":"Traditional versus Roth deferrals affect a current household differently","body":["Traditional 401(k) salary deferrals typically reduce current federal taxable wages, while qualified distributions are generally taxable as ordinary income later. Designated Roth 401(k) contributions use after-tax income and generally provide tax-free qualified distributions when the applicable five-year and qualifying event conditions are met. The economic comparison must hold after-tax saving cost constant; otherwise one can appear larger merely because one used more of the household's current disposable income.","For a $3,000 employee contribution at a stable 12% marginal ordinary federal rate, the basic traditional current federal income-tax effect is roughly $360. A $3,000 Roth contribution usually provides no equivalent immediate income-tax deduction. The invested amount may be the same, but the immediate net paycheck reduction can differ. Traditional money may generate later income tax and possible RMDs; Roth money may avoid those taxes when distribution conditions are met.","A work plan's choice between two tax treatments does not change the value of emergency cash. A family can choose Roth for retirement but still fail a home-closing liquidity test; high future tax uncertainty does not justify locking away rent payments. Tax brackets, employer match taxation, expected future income, contribution limits and possible change of employment need separate line items."],"worked":["Qualified traditional deferral $3,000 at a stable illustrative 12% federal marginal rate ⇒ current tax reduction ~$360.","A $3,000 Roth deferral provides $0 current federal income-tax reduction under ordinary assumptions.","Traditional net paycheck effect ≈$2,640, Roth ≈$3,000, ignoring payroll/tax interactions.","Compare eventual qualified Roth distributions against future traditional taxes, not only today's paycheck."],"practice":[["Does a 12% federal bracket make Roth always worse than traditional?","No. A contribution's best tax treatment depends on future withdrawal rates, legal eligibility, lifetime income, liquidity, time horizon and uncertainty."],["Why is a workplace Roth deferral generally not subtracted from W-2 Box 1 wages?","It is an after-tax contribution for income-tax purposes; qualified distributions can receive future tax-free treatment rather than an immediate wage exclusion."]]},{"title":"Plan funds, fees and fiduciary responsibilities matter as much as contribution rates","body":["Once the employee makes a contribution, they still need an investment policy. A target-date fund commonly reduces stock exposure with age, but its glide path, fees, bond duration, global diversification and allocation philosophy vary. Two '2050' funds may have materially different portfolios. An index fund can offer transparent exposure at low cost, yet its underlying market risk remains. Company stock can concentrate both wage and investment risk in one employer.","The annual participant fee disclosure generally identifies investment expense ratios and plan-level administration fees. An extra 1% annual cost on $100,000 is approximately $1,000 during a year before investment changes; over decades, compounding can produce a much larger wealth difference. A fee comparison requires the same gross-market return and asset allocation, rather than contrasting a low-fee stock index fund against a high-fee capital-guaranteed product with different risk.","ERISA fiduciary duties typically require plan fiduciaries to act prudently and in participants' interests, but not all plans are identical and not every recommendation in a workplace is fiduciary advice. Check the plan's Form 5500, summary plan description, investment menu and fees where applicable. Document conflicts, investment restrictions and whether better alternatives exist."],"worked":["Hypothetical $40,000 balance with 0.10% expense ratio costs ~$40 per year before balance changes.","Same balance at 1.10% costs ~$440 per year under simple static-balance approximation.","Difference ~$400 annually before compounding and asset performance.","A fee-efficient plan still needs proper asset allocation for goals, age, and loss capacity."],"practice":[["Is a target-date fund the same as an insured savings account?","No. It generally holds investments that can lose value and its glide path and fees vary by provider."],["Why can concentrated employer stock be more risky than a simple portfolio chart suggests?","The employee's wages, job security and retirement wealth can all fall with one company; economic exposures are correlated."]]},{"title":"Loans, hardship distributions, rollovers and job changes","body":["Some workplace plans permit participant loans up to limits commonly tied to 50% of the vested balance and a $50,000 ceiling, with exceptions and plan-specific rules. A properly administered loan generally must be repaid, often within five years unless the primary residence exception applies. An outstanding balance that defaults or becomes a deemed distribution can generate income tax and possible additional tax. Calling a 401(k) loan 'tax-free home funding' ignores repayment obligations and employment-related risk.","Hardship distributions can be permitted for immediate and heavy needs under plan rules, but they are not generally repaid to the plan, reduce future retirement wealth, and may create income tax and a 10% early-distribution additional tax unless an exception applies. Different penalty exceptions apply to IRAs versus employer plans. The first-time-homebuyer IRA exception, for example, does not automatically make an early 401(k) distribution tax-free or penalty-free.","After a job change, leaving savings in an old plan, a direct trustee-to-trustee rollover to a qualified new plan or IRA, and a potentially taxable cash-out carry different fees, creditor protection, distribution access, required minimum distributions and beneficiary consequences. Avoid casually routing funds through a personal bank account, which can create withholding and rollover-timing complications. Always read the plan and current IRS distribution guidance."],"worked":["Vested account $40,000: generic potential loan 50%×$40,000 = $20,000, below $50,000 cap, if plan permits.","This is a liability requiring repayments, not an additional $20,000 of wealth.","If $8,000 of a previously untaxed hardship distribution is taxable at 22%, simple federal tax effect $1,760.","A possible 10% additional tax could add $800 absent an exception, plus state taxes and forgone growth."],"practice":[["Why is a workplace plan loan different from taking cash from taxable brokerage savings?","A plan loan imposes repayment obligations and can become taxable if it fails qualification or repayment rules; it also restricts invested retirement capital."],["Why should a new employee not request a 'cash-out' before understanding rollover options?","A taxable distribution can permanently reduce retirement savings and trigger current tax, additional penalties and missed compound growth; direct rollover alternatives may preserve benefits."]]},{"title":"Independent employee benefits audit: from documents to a retirement policy","body":["A university-level benefits audit is a reproducible packet rather than a single percentage suggestion. It begins with the annual compensation definition, plan eligibility and enrollment date, deferral election, match schedule, true-up policy, vesting credited service, investment menu, all-in fees, beneficiaries and early-distribution rules. Attach screenshots or summaries with identifying information removed in the educational workspace.","Prepare three strategies for a household that can potentially save $250, $375 or $500 per month but must preserve its emergency fund and upcoming housing costs. At $60,000 eligible salary and 50% of first 6% matching, 6% employee deferral means $300 a month and full $150 monthly employer match under an annual approximation. A $250 budget falls short of the full 6% employee contribution unless other expenses are adjusted; increasing the deferral without available cash would create a deficit.","Conclude with a plan to revisit allocations and employer terms every year, whenever the family changes jobs, and when it receives plan amendments. Investment education should not guarantee employer match permanence, returns, tax treatment or access to the money. A defendable recommendation states what to verify before the employer's next payroll deadline."],"worked":["Income $60,000 ⇒ eligible monthly compensation $5,000.","Full 6% match capture employee contribution $300/month; employer match $150/month.","At $250/month employee contribution =5% of eligible pay; employer match 50%×5%×$5,000=$125/month.","Extra $50 per month employee contribution could capture extra $25/month match if cash constraints and plan terms permit."],"practice":[["Why is recommending 'contribute at least 6%' incomplete in this household?","The full match threshold may cost $300 monthly against $250 available, potentially undermining essential bills. Budget capacity and plan eligibility must be checked."],["What must be included in a fiduciary-style plan review memo?","Match formula, vesting, deferral limits, fees, liquidity restrictions, investments, plan documents, practical affordability and ongoing review triggers."]]}],"sourceRefs":[["IRS 2026 plan and IRA contribution limit announcement","https://www.irs.gov/newsroom/401k-limit-increases-to-24500-for-2026-ira-limit-increases-to-7500"],["IRS 2026 limits for 401(k) and profit sharing plans","https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-participant-employee/retirement-topics-401k-and-profit-sharing-plan-contribution-limits"],["IRS 2026 catch-ups and mandatory Roth catch-up threshold","https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-participant-employee/retirement-topics-catch-up-contributions"],["IRS employee and employer vesting rights","https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-participant-employee/retirement-topics-vesting"],["IRS employer match vesting schedules","https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/issue-snapshot-vesting-schedules-for-matching-contributions"],["IRS general 401(k) distribution and loan rules","https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-participant-employee/401k-resource-guide-plan-participants-general-distribution-rules"],["IRS hardship distribution consequences","https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/401k-plan-hardship-distributions-consider-the-consequences"],["US Department of Labor ERISA plan fee disclosure","https://www.dol.gov/agencies/ebsa/key-topics/retirement/401k-plans/fee-disclosure"],["Investor.gov asset allocation and rebalancing","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/getting-started/asset-allocation"]],"assessment":{"title":"Employee benefits due diligence and match optimization","prompt":"For a fictional Connecticut worker earning $60,000, compare 4%, 5%, 6%, and 10% employee deferrals under 50% employer matching on the first 6% of eligible compensation. Calculate annual and monthly employee and employer contributions; repeat under 24 paychecks with 6 periods having no employee contribution and no true-up. Assume a separately stated $12,000 employee source and $8,000 employer source, 40% vested; calculate vested amount and forfeiture exposure. Model current federal tax effect of a $3,000 traditional versus Roth deferral at a stable illustrative 12% bracket. Write a 600–900-word memo recommending a contribution policy under a $250/month current cash budget and a $6,000 protected emergency reserve.","rubric":["Employer match formula and 24-paycheck reconciliation (25%)","Ownership, vesting and credited service (20%)","2026 statutory and plan limit distinctions (15%)","Traditional/Roth tax and household cash-flow integration (20%)","Investment governance, document traceability and ethics (20%)"],"answerKey":["At employee 4/5/6/10% of $60k: deferrals $2,400/$3,000/$3,600/$6,000 and matches $1,200/$1,500/$1,800/$1,800.","At $2,500/paycheck and full 6% deferral: $150 employee and $75 employer each payroll. Eighteen matched periods with no true-up => $1,350 match, $450 below a fully matched 24-period year.","Employee vested $12,000, employer vested $3,200, total $15,200; $4,800 employer amount potentially unvested.","At stable 12% ordinary federal rate, hypothetical eligible $3,000 traditional deferral saves about $360 current federal regular income tax; equivalent Roth contribution has no upfront federal deduction.","A $250/month budget is insufficient to contribute 6% of $60k, or $300/month, without additional funds. Do not promise a full match while omitting rent and reserves."]}},{"id":"L12.S2","title":"Traditional and Roth Accounts","subtitle":"Tax location, IRA eligibility, equal-cost comparisons, conversion taxation and lifetime account design","prerequisites":["L11.S1 income and adjusted-gross-income calculations","L11.S2 marginal versus effective tax rate","L12.S1 workplace plan design and contribution limits","L7.S3 compound growth and present value"],"objectives":["Compare traditional versus Roth saving at equal after-tax household cost","Apply 2026 IRA combined limits, compensation rules and income phaseouts","Distinguish Roth IRA from designated Roth 401(k) eligibility and distributions","Analyze rollover, conversion, Form 8606 and aggregation pitfalls","Quantify future net retirement value under alternative tax scenarios","Explain Roth distribution ordering, five-year conditions and early-withdrawal risks","Evaluate multi-account tax diversification for family and estate objectives"],"sections":[{"title":"Retirement tax treatment is a timeline, not a label of 'tax free'","body":["A traditional retirement account generally defers income taxation: eligible contributions may reduce taxable income now, investment income can compound without current annual tax inside the account, and later pretax distributions are generally ordinary taxable income. Roth arrangements generally invest after-tax dollars and can offer income-tax-free qualified withdrawals. Neither system guarantees an investment return, and neither automatically makes the account liquid for emergencies.","An employer Roth 401(k) and a personal Roth IRA use similar names but different income eligibility, five-year distribution and plan administration rules. Income limits can restrict direct Roth IRA contributions, while designated Roth 401(k) elective deferrals generally do not use Roth IRA modified-AGI contribution restrictions. Employer contributions are separately tracked and may be taxable or Roth according to permitted plan elections and the latest plan rules. Tax sources need explicit labeling.","Choosing a wrapper is distinct from choosing assets. A Roth IRA holding a highly concentrated individual stock remains risky; a traditional retirement account holding a low-cost diversified fund may be appropriate for a different goal. The household's objective should be stated in after-tax dollars available at retirement, including distribution constraints."],"worked":["Traditional pre-tax $5,000 contribution at stable 22% marginal tax can save about $1,100 current federal ordinary income tax.","Roth $5,000 eligible contribution generally has no immediate deduction.","Both may invest the same dollars but require different current after-tax cash outlay.","Future qualified Roth withdrawals can be excluded from federal income tax; pretax traditional distributions are ordinarily taxable."],"practice":[["Does 'Roth account' guarantee that every early withdrawal is tax free and penalty free?","No. Qualification and ordering depend on account type, holding period, age and whether the withdrawn sum is a contribution, conversion or earnings."],["Why do account tax labels not determine asset risk?","Tax treatment changes the timing of taxes, not the return distribution or concentration risk of the securities held inside the account."]]},{"title":"Equal after-tax savings is the fair traditional versus Roth comparison","body":["Comparing $3,000 traditional against $3,000 Roth often ignores the traditional tax saving. Suppose a worker can reduce current take-home cash by $3,000, the marginal federal rate today is 22%, and a fully deductible traditional contribution is permitted. That after-tax cost could support $3,000 ÷ (1−0.22) ≈ $3,846.15 of gross traditional saving under a simplified constant-rate model, versus $3,000 Roth. Both represent the same current cash sacrifice, not the same account deposit.","Assume the funds compound for 20 years at a fixed illustrative 6% annual rate with no fees. If the future traditional distribution faces 12% federal ordinary tax and Roth earnings qualify, future after-tax traditional value is $3,846.15 × 1.06^20 × 0.88, while Roth becomes $3,000 × 1.06^20. The traditional approach wins in this controlled example because the tax rate later is lower; with the same 22% current/future rate, after-tax values are equal before other rules. Change the rate, liquidity, fee and investment assumptions and the decision may reverse.","Employer match, IRA deductibility, credit phaseouts, Medicare premiums, state taxes, future RMDs and Social Security taxation can modify this simple result. Comparison must explicitly state whether a refund or tax saving is reinvested or spent. A recommendation that credits the traditional contribution for its tax deduction yet assumes a Roth contribution of equal gross amount uses unequal budgets."],"worked":["Current after-tax saving capacity $3,000; 22% tax today.","Gross traditional equivalent $3,000/(1−0.22)≈$3,846.15; Roth deposits $3,000.","20-year growth factor 1.06^20≈3.207135.","At 12% future traditional rate: trad after-tax ≈$10,855, Roth ≈$9,621, before other costs and taxes."],"practice":[["Why is comparing equal gross contributions not always a fair tax-vehicle comparison?","The traditional contribution can create current tax savings, making its net cash cost lower. An equal-after-tax-cost model controls for that advantage."],["When do equal-cost traditional and Roth portfolios produce the same after-tax value under simplifying assumptions?","If the contribution is fully deductible, Roth distributions qualify, tax rates at contribution and withdrawal are equal, and investment/fee assumptions match."]]},{"title":"2026 IRA limits and how compensation determines eligibility","body":["For 2026, the traditional and Roth IRA combined regular contribution limit is $7,500 for an eligible individual under age 50, and $8,600 for an eligible individual age 50 or older, reflecting a $1,100 catch-up. These are combined limits: $5,000 Roth plus $2,500 traditional already fills the $7,500 limit. IRA contributions generally cannot exceed eligible taxable compensation, though special spousal IRA provisions can allow contributions based on compensation on a joint return.","The traditional IRA deduction is not always available simply because a contribution is allowed. When the contributor is covered by an employer retirement plan, the 2026 deduction phases out at modified AGI $81,000–$91,000 for single/head-of-household filers or $129,000–$149,000 married filing jointly. A married person not covered by a work plan but with a covered spouse can encounter a separate $242,000–$252,000 range. Income-phaseout arithmetic can require official worksheets and legal eligibility checks.","Direct Roth IRA contributions have their own 2026 modified-AGI phaseouts: $153,000–$168,000 for single/head-of-household and $242,000–$252,000 for married joint filers, with special separate-filing rules. These income limits do not impose an additional cap on contributions to an employer Roth 401(k) when the workplace plan permits that feature. Avoid combining the two as though they are one account."],"worked":["Single age 42 2026 eligible IRA maximum $7,500, subject to sufficient compensation and modified-AGI rules.","Age 52 2026 eligible limit $7,500+$1,100=$8,600.","$3,000 traditional IRA+$4,500 Roth IRA uses the shared $7,500 under-50 contribution ceiling.","A single filer at $100,000 MAGI may lose a traditional IRA deduction if covered at work, while income-limited direct Roth eligibility is a separate question."],"practice":[["If someone contributes $7,500 to a traditional IRA, can they also put $7,500 into a Roth IRA for 2026 at age 42?","No. The $7,500 annual under-50 contribution limit generally applies to the combined traditional and Roth IRA contributions, subject to earned compensation."],["Does a nondeductible traditional IRA contribution mean the contribution is prohibited?","Not necessarily. IRA contribution eligibility and the deductibility of an otherwise eligible traditional contribution are separate determinations."]]},{"title":"Roth IRA distribution ordering, five-year rules and early withdrawals","body":["Roth IRA regular contributions are generally considered distributed before conversions and earnings under statutory ordering rules. As a result, withdrawing amounts corresponding to regular Roth IRA contributions can have different tax treatment from distributing appreciation. Conversion dollars follow their own ordering and potentially separate five-year penalty clocks. A tax-year-based five-year rule applies to making distributions qualified, and qualifying conditions generally include age 59½, death, disability or limited first-home provisions.","The IRS makes distinctions that a one-line 'Roth is tax free' slogan conceals. A 30-year-old who directly contributed $5,000 and later has $8,000 total could generally withdraw up to $5,000 of regular contributions without the same income-tax treatment as $3,000 of earnings, assuming accurate records and no competing distributions. Early withdrawal of earnings may be taxable and can incur a 10% additional tax absent an exception. A converted amount distributed too early can have its own additional-tax implications.","Roth 401(k) nonqualified distribution ordering is not the same as Roth IRA regular-contribution ordering; an employer plan distribution can include a pro-rata earnings portion. Direct rollovers and carefully tracked dates matter. Never recommend using retirement funds for a home down payment based solely on generic withdrawal language; determine the precise account type and facts."],"worked":["Roth IRA regular contributions to date $5,000; total value $8,000 before withdrawals.","Simplified distribution $4,000 first from regular-contribution layer leaves remaining regular contribution layer $1,000.","A further $3,000 withdrawal can reach conversion/earnings layers depending on account history.","Tax and penalty assessment requires a complete transaction and conversion history; a single balance cannot establish the result."],"practice":[["Why should an adviser ask whether the account is a Roth IRA or Roth 401(k) before discussing an early withdrawal?","The tax ordering, qualified distribution conditions and handling of nonqualified earnings differ across account types."],["Can someone assume a new Roth IRA's $3,000 investment profit is withdrawable tax free after one year?","No. Earnings need qualified-distribution conditions for tax-free treatment; early nonqualified withdrawals may create taxes and additional penalties."]]},{"title":"Roth conversions change tax character, not total economic wealth overnight","body":["A Roth conversion shifts eligible pretax traditional IRA or plan money into Roth tax treatment, generally recognizing income tax on previously untaxed value in the conversion year. The converted asset does not instantly increase in economic value: it is still the same underlying property in a different tax wrapper, with a tax liability paid from the conversion or other resources. When paying tax from outside funds, the household is adding a larger total after-tax cash sacrifice than the IRA balance screenshot suggests.","Consider converting $20,000 of entirely pretax traditional IRA assets in a year when the conversion's incremental ordinary federal tax is 12%, producing about $2,400 current federal tax under a stable bracket assumption. If the same conversion were marginally taxed at 24%, the simple federal tax becomes $4,800. The true result can cross brackets, affect taxable Social Security, Medicare IRMAA, ACA subsidies or Connecticut taxes. Form 8606 and other forms may be required.","Conversions are often most attractive when a temporary low-income year creates unusually low marginal rates, or when long-run estate and RMD considerations justify paying tax earlier. They can also backfire when the tax must be borrowed at high rates, the conversion inflates current costs, or the household needs cash for a home purchase. A serious plan compares 'convert' to 'do not convert' under multiple future tax-rate and mortality scenarios."],"worked":["Convert $20,000 fully pretax at simplified stable 12% marginal federal rate: additional federal tax $2,400.","At a stable 24% marginal rate the same amount implies $4,800 additional federal income tax.","A conversion from an account with after-tax basis may have a partially nontaxable portion calculated under aggregation rules.","The converted $20,000 does not create extra net worth simply through retitling as Roth."],"practice":[["Is a $20,000 Roth conversion automatically a $20,000 federal taxable addition?","No. The taxable portion depends on pretax and after-tax basis and applicable conversion rules, including traditional IRA aggregation and Form 8606."],["Why can paying conversion tax out of an emergency reserve undermine an otherwise tax-efficient plan?","It sacrifices necessary liquidity and can force costly borrowing; the conversion's expected long-term benefit must justify a real immediate cash cost."]]},{"title":"Nondeductible basis, Form 8606 and the IRA pro-rata trap","body":["A nondeductible traditional IRA contribution establishes after-tax basis that must be tracked so it is not taxed again when distributed or converted. Form 8606 is central to this recordkeeping. When a taxpayer has both after-tax basis and pretax balances across traditional, SEP and SIMPLE IRAs, the tax calculation generally aggregates eligible IRA balances, instead of allowing the taxpayer to select only a newly funded nondeductible lot for conversion.","For a clean mathematical illustration, suppose total traditional/SEP/SIMPLE IRA value relevant to the simplified year-end pro-rata calculation plus the amount converted is $100,000, and $10,000 is nondeductible basis. If the owner converts $10,000, the nontaxable fraction is approximately 10%, making $1,000 tax-free basis recovery and $9,000 taxable conversion. The remaining basis is preserved for later calculations. The exact annual Form 8606 includes specific adjusted-value and distribution inputs.","Calling this sequence 'backdoor Roth' does not change the legal calculation. A pre-existing IRA rollover can create a substantial pro-rata taxable conversion unexpectedly. Direct trustee transfers, pretax IRA rollovers to an eligible workplace plan when allowed, conversion timing and any 2026 applicable limits require individual documentation and professional review. This is not a guaranteed way around income tax."],"worked":["Aggregate hypothetical denominator $100,000; nondeductible basis $10,000 ⇒ basis ratio 10%.","Convert $10,000: nontaxable portion $1,000, taxable portion $9,000.","At simplified 22% marginal rate, current federal tax on taxable $9,000 ≈$1,980.","Remaining nondeductible IRA basis after the conversion ≈$9,000, subject to actual Form 8606 rules."],"practice":[["Why is simply converting a newly contributed $10,000 nondeductible IRA amount not necessarily tax-free?","Other existing pretax IRA balances can enter the aggregate pro-rata calculation, making much of the nominal $10,000 conversion taxable."],["What financial records are required to avoid double taxation of nondeductible IRA contributions?","A history of nondeductible IRA basis and Forms 8606, plus distributions, conversions, rollovers and year-end IRA values."]]},{"title":"Asset location and tax diversification among accounts","body":["Asset allocation describes total stock, bond and cash exposures, while asset location describes which exposures are held in taxable brokerage, traditional retirement or Roth accounts. The location can alter timing and character of taxes. Taxable bond interest commonly creates annual taxable income in a brokerage account, while a broad equity index fund may distribute relatively little gain before shares are sold. A Roth account can shelter long-run compounding from regular federal income tax when withdrawals qualify.","The location decision is constrained by actual account access and beneficiary needs. A 30-year home purchase fund belongs in investments matched to its short horizon rather than in aggressive equities simply because Roth long-run returns sound tax-advantaged. Traditional retirement withdrawals may increase later adjusted gross income and interact with Social Security tax and Medicare costs. Roth balances can help manage taxable income later, but they do not eliminate market risk.","Some holdings have their own special tax rules: Treasury interest, municipal funds, REIT distributions and foreign tax credits cannot be assigned a one-size location without checking fund holdings, state residency and tax character. An academic comparison lists the total household after-tax value and accounts for fees, constraints, liquidity and the cost of accessing each account."],"worked":["Hypothetical taxable interest-bearing assets $10,000 yielding 4% ⇒ $400 interest before federal/state tax.","At a stable illustrative 22% federal marginal rate, tax on that interest ≈$88 before state effects.","A qualifying Roth account generally does not tax annual internal interest in the same current way; eventual distribution rules govern.","Tax-efficiency alone cannot justify taking 30% stock drawdown risk on next year's down payment."],"practice":[["How does asset location differ from asset allocation?","Allocation chooses overall economic risk exposures; location chooses which tax and legal account holds each exposure."],["What limitation prevents automatically placing every growth asset in a Roth account?","Contribution limits, taxes on conversions, estate goals, liquidity, diversification and future risk needs constrain what can be placed there."]]},{"title":"Connecticut retirement-income treatment is a second independent calculation","body":["Connecticut state treatment of pensions and IRA distributions can differ materially from the federal return. A 2025 Connecticut General Assembly research report describes a pension/annuity subtraction subject to adjusted-gross-income eligibility and phaseout, and an IRA distribution subtraction rising to 100% in 2026 for qualifying taxpayers under the same income thresholds and phaseout schedule. Federal taxes can still apply even if the state subtraction eliminates the Connecticut component.","For a typical married joint filer with federal adjusted gross income below $100,000, qualifying pension or annuity income can be eligible for a full Connecticut subtraction, subject to statutory definitions. Above that threshold, an income-dependent phaseout can extend until the benefit is eliminated by $150,000 joint AGI. The corresponding single-filer full threshold is $75,000 with phaseout generally to $100,000. Connecticut's current DRS calculators and final tax-year instructions must govern the actual return.","These rules make a Roth conversion decision especially fact-sensitive for someone expecting to retire in Connecticut. A conversion raises current federal taxable income and can affect Connecticut AGI and income-tested subtractions. Before promising any tax savings, model both jurisdictions, the person's actual filing status and income level, and other modifications. A legislative proposal is not an enacted law."],"worked":["Assumed Connecticut MFJ federal AGI $90,000 and a qualifying taxable pension distribution $12,000 already included in AGI.","Under current threshold-based illustration, that distribution may be eligible for full state subtraction, subject to definition and return rules.","The federal ordinary income tax on the same distribution can remain positive.","If conversion raises federal AGI above the state threshold, recalculate state subtraction and any phaseout; don't use a fixed marginal estimate."],"practice":[["Does Connecticut's pension subtraction remove the pension income from federal adjusted gross income?","No. The state subtraction is generally a Connecticut-specific modification after federal income reporting, not a revision to the federal tax calculation."],["Why is a 2026 Roth conversion different for a Connecticut retiree with income near a phaseout threshold?","Added conversion income can increase federal AGI and change eligibility for Connecticut retirement-income subtractions or other income-tested provisions."]]},{"title":"The lifetime account map and beneficiary transition","body":["Build a balance-sheet map of retirement accounts with owner, custodian, pretax amount, after-tax basis, Roth five-year dates, employer vesting and designated beneficiaries. A 401(k) statement and an IRA account statement may have different ownership and inherited-account rules. Beneficiary designation often governs where retirement money goes at death; a will or revocable trust does not automatically override every qualified plan or IRA designation.","Non-spouse beneficiaries of retirement accounts may be subject to a ten-year distribution requirement, with annual distributions in some circumstances and special rules for eligible designated beneficiaries. Spouses have options that many other beneficiaries do not. The timing of inherited pretax distributions can create significant income tax for heirs. Contributing to a Roth does not necessarily mean a beneficiary can ignore all distribution deadlines.","The educational objective is a legally coherent household retirement map, not a trick for putting an account 'inside' a trust to escape taxes. The family must coordinate designated beneficiaries, marriage rights, trust language, required distributions, incapacity planning and estate goals with legal/tax professionals. A living account owner's unrestricted choice does not mean beneficiaries can later ignore statutory rules."],"worked":["Identify $50,000 pretax 401(k), $10,000 Roth IRA regular contributions and $5,000 taxable brokerage.","Total visible assets $65,000, but current access and future tax differ by account.","For a taxable inherited pretax account, the time and manner of distributions can create beneficiary ordinary taxable income.","Estate recommendation must identify actual account forms, designated beneficiaries and applicable death-year distribution rules."],"practice":[["Can a trust be named beneficiary without checking retirement plan and tax rules?","The designation may be permitted but trust terms, documentation, look-through status and required distribution rules can materially change outcomes; specialist coordination is essential."],["Do Roth accounts always remain entirely undistributed to heirs forever?","No. Qualified Roth tax treatment does not eliminate beneficiary distribution requirements, including potential ten-year distribution rules."]]}],"sourceRefs":[["IRS October 2026 IRA education bulletin","https://www.irs.gov/newsroom/saving-for-retirement-iras-are-an-option"],["IRS 2026 IRA contribution and Roth income phaseout details","https://www.irs.gov/newsroom/401k-limit-increases-to-24500-for-2026-ira-limit-increases-to-7500"],["IRS IRA contribution limits and compensation rules","https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-participant-employee/retirement-topics-ira-contribution-limits"],["IRS Publication 590-A, IRA contributions and conversion mechanics","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p590a"],["IRS Publication 590-B, withdrawals and Roth ordering","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p590b"],["IRS Form 8606 for nondeductible IRA basis and conversions","https://www.irs.gov/instructions/i8606"],["IRS designated Roth accounts","https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-participant-employee/retirement-topics-designated-roth-account"],["Connecticut OLR retirement-income exemptions 2025-R-0152","https://www.cga.ct.gov/2025/rpt/pdf/2025-R-0152.pdf"],["Connecticut DRS senior tax guidance","https://portal.ct.gov/drs/individuals/connecticut-tax-tips-for-senior-citizens"],["IRS beneficiary distribution rules","https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-participant-employee/retirement-topics-beneficiary"]],"assessment":{"title":"Equal-cost account policy, 2026 eligibility and conversion scenario","prompt":"For a 42-year-old Connecticut worker, compare a $3,000 AFTER-TAX household saving cost invested as qualified Roth versus fully deductible traditional at 22% current federal marginal tax for 20 years at a smooth 6% hypothetical return. Compute future outcomes at 12%, 22%, and 32% future withdrawal tax rates, stating modeling limits. Independently calculate 2026 combined IRA contribution capacity, then illustrate a $10,000 IRA conversion from $100,000 of aggregate pretax/aftertax IRA assets containing $10,000 nondeductible basis. Write a two-page planning memo about $6,000 emergency reserves, a future house, state subtraction eligibility and beneficiary paperwork.","rubric":["Equal after-tax-cost comparison and sensitivity (25%)","2026 contribution, compensation and income phaseouts (20%)","Form 8606 and pro-rata conversion calculations (20%)","Roth ordering and distribution limitations (15%)","Connecticut, liquidity and beneficiary planning (20%)"],"answerKey":["Equal cost $3,000 Roth vs traditional $3,000/(1−.22)=$3,846.153846 gross contribution.","Assume 20 years at 6%: growth factor 3.207135; Roth value about $9,621.40, while traditional gross value about $12,335.13 before future tax.","At 12% future tax traditional after-tax ≈$10,854.91; 22% ≈$9,621.40; 32% ≈$8,387.89 (rounding based on precise factor).","2026 IRA joint traditional+Roth total limit under age 50 $7,500 or eligible compensation if less; age 50+ generally $8,600.","Pro-rata: 10% nondeductible basis on $10k conversion gives $1,000 nontaxable and $9,000 taxable, subject to actual Form 8606 inputs.","The memo preserves essential near-term reserves and distinguishes federal income tax, CT state subtractions, withdrawal qualification and the ten-year beneficiary context."]}},{"id":"L12.S3","title":"Social Security and Retirement Income","subtitle":"Lifetime benefit formula, claiming age, earnings test, tax interaction, longevity and Connecticut household coordination","prerequisites":["L7.S3 time value of money and cash-flow timelines","L11.S1 taxable-income reconciliation","L11.S2 progressive federal rates","L12.S2 traditional versus Roth distribution mechanics"],"objectives":["Explain Social Security covered earnings, 35-year indexing and progressive PIA formula","Compare claiming at 62, full retirement age, and 70 with dated cohorts","Calculate gross cumulative break-even ages without misrepresenting uncertainty","Apply 2026 SSA retirement earnings test and subsequent FRA recalculation caveat","Explain federal taxation of benefits and Connecticut-specific adjustments","Model inflation, spouse/survivor considerations and Medicare enrollment","Build a household retirement-income bridge covering savings, pensions and benefits"],"sections":[{"title":"Social Security is social insurance, not an individual brokerage balance","body":["Social Security retirement benefits are based primarily on the worker's covered lifetime earnings and the claiming age, not on a private account in which contributions purchase shares. Payroll taxes help finance a social insurance system that also pays survivors and disability benefits. An individual's tax contributions, payroll history and future benefit are related by law but do not create a separate portfolio statement. This distinction matters when comparing retirement savings accounts to government benefits.","The Social Security Administration typically selects the worker's highest 35 years of covered wage earnings after applying wage indexing where required, divides indexed annual earnings into average indexed monthly earnings (AIME), and applies a progressive primary insurance amount (PIA) formula. Years with no covered earnings can enter as zeros and lower the average; replacing a zero with a new substantial work year can increase future PIA. A worker with more than 35 years of earnings may improve the average by replacing a lower indexed year.","Social Security statements provide personalized estimates based on SSA records and assumed future earnings. They are not guaranteed account statements; law, recorded wages, claim date and family benefits can alter the result. The learner should verify their earnings history and use an SSA official account or estimator rather than treating an average national monthly benefit as their own."],"worked":["Hypothetical 35-year indexed earnings total $1,680,000.","AIME = $1,680,000/(35×12)=$4,000/month.","A missing year counted as zero affects the numerator without reducing the 420-month denominator.","Use the worker's actual SSA earnings statement to validate the calculation; not every salary is covered equally."],"practice":[["Why are two workers with equal current salaries not guaranteed equal Social Security benefits?","Lifetime covered earnings, indexing, years of zero or low earnings, claiming age and other eligibility rules can produce different benefits."],["Is Social Security payment tied to the current market value of a personal tax-funded equity account?","No. Retirement benefits are statutory social insurance calculated from covered earnings and claiming conditions, not investment shares in a personal account."]]},{"title":"AIME, bend points and the 2026 PIA formula","body":["The PIA formula is progressive: it replaces a higher percentage of low averaged indexed earnings than high averaged indexed earnings. For workers first eligible in 2026, SSA lists bend points of $1,286 and $7,749. The gross monthly formula before special adjustments is 90% of the first $1,286 of AIME, plus 32% of AIME above $1,286 through $7,749, plus 15% above $7,749. The PIA is subsequently rounded and can be affected by indexing/eligibility timing and other statutory provisions.","For the fictional $4,000 AIME worker, a simplified unrounded PIA is 0.9×$1,286 + 0.32×($4,000−$1,286) = $1,157.40+$868.48=$2,025.88. This is a 2026 formula illustration, not an individualized monthly benefit quote. Delayed retirement, early claiming, COLAs, family maximums and benefit adjustments all occur later in the modeling chain.","The formula shows why one extra dollar of AIME does not translate to one extra dollar of monthly PIA. In the first segment, marginal benefit is approximately 90 cents per dollar of monthly AIME, in the middle 32 cents, and in the upper segment 15 cents. Because AIME is itself a 35-year average, additional covered wages in a particular late-career year can have a smaller or zero impact if they do not enter the highest 35 years."],"worked":["SSA 2026 eligibility bend points $1,286 and $7,749.","For AIME $4,000, 90%×$1,286=$1,157.40.","32%×($4,000−$1,286)=32%×$2,714=$868.48.","Illustrative unrounded PIA $2,025.88/month; actual SSA computation has statutory rounding and further rules."],"practice":[["Why does an added dollar of annual salary not automatically increase PIA by a full dollar?","It may or may not enter the 35 highest indexed earning years, then is averaged over 420 months and multiplied by a progressive PIA formula."],["What do 2026 bend points represent?","They are thresholds of average indexed monthly earnings used in computing the initial primary insurance amount for an applicable eligibility cohort, not gross salary tax brackets."]]},{"title":"The claim-age choice changes lifetime cash-flow timing","body":["For workers born in 1960 or later, full retirement age is 67. An eligible worker's benefit at age 62 is generally approximately 70% of their PIA and at age 70 roughly 124% of PIA, absent other adjustments. Claiming early gives more years of smaller checks; delaying gives fewer initial years but a larger ongoing benefit. Neither path always wins because mortality, taxes, household survivors, investment opportunity cost and health differ.","Let a fictional worker have $2,000 monthly PIA at full retirement age. The simplified comparisons are $1,400/month at 62, $2,000 at 67 and $2,480 at 70. These are constant-dollar starting illustrations using current claiming percentages, not an SSA personal forecast or account guarantee. A person who cannot work until age 70 could fund the delay from savings, requiring a bridge that uses money and faces investment and inflation risk.","The decision often depends on a spouse and survivor situation. For a couple, delaying the higher earner's benefit can be more valuable because the higher benefit may influence what a surviving spouse can receive. However, spousal and survivor formulas differ from worker retirement reduction charts, and the appropriate order of claiming should be modeled on actual ages, health and SSA benefit records."],"worked":["Hypothetical FRA age 67 monthly PIA $2,000.","Age 62 ≈70%×$2,000=$1,400/month.","Age 67 ≈$2,000/month; age 70 ≈124%×$2,000=$2,480/month.","Annual nominal checks at those rates: $16,800, $24,000 and $29,760 (before tax and inflation)."],"practice":[["Does claiming at age 70 always produce more total lifetime benefits?","No. Monthly benefit is higher but payments start later, so the outcome depends on longevity, inflation, taxes, opportunity cost and household benefits."],["Why should a married couple model the higher earner's claiming age separately?","The decision can affect survivor income and lifetime household security, not merely the higher earner's individual break-even age."]]},{"title":"Break-even ages are useful but incomplete mathematics","body":["A gross cash-flow break-even calculation asks when the cumulative checks from a later claiming strategy catch up with those from an earlier strategy. It is not a return forecast. Compare a 62 claim of $1,400 per month with a 70 claim of $2,480. The earlier claimant receives eight years of checks before the delayed claimant begins: 96×$1,400=$134,400. After age 70 the monthly difference is $1,080, so catching up takes $134,400/$1,080≈124.44 months, or roughly age 80 years and 4 months.","This gross comparison assumes identical constant dollars, no discounting, no benefit taxes, no SSA recalculation, no survivor effect and no specific cost-of-living adjustment differences. A present-value comparison discounts each payment by an appropriate uncertain rate and tests lifespan scenarios. Applying the same nominal discount rate to COLA-adjusted versus non-COLA cash flows without making units consistent is a common error.","An advanced model plots cumulative cash receipts, then separately after-tax present value and a household survivor scenario. At age 75, age 62 claiming may have produced more cash than age 70; at age 90, the higher age 70 benefit may dominate the simplified nominal cumulative receipts. The objective is to understand which assumption causes a reversal, not to announce a universal claiming age."],"worked":["Age 62 claim: 96 months ×$1,400 = $134,400 received before age 70.","At age 70, monthly late-claim benefit $2,480 versus early $1,400 ⇒ difference $1,080.","Break-even months after 70 = $134,400/$1,080≈124.44.","Approximate gross break-even age 80.37, ignoring discounting, COLA, taxes and other benefits."],"practice":[["What does a simple gross break-even age omit?","Discounting, changes in earnings and benefits, federal/state tax, inflation, survival probabilities, survivor rights and opportunity costs of bridge assets."],["Can two equally reasoned households choose different claiming ages?","Yes. Longevity, spouse coverage, income needs, insurance and balance-sheet constraints alter the financial value of later versus earlier benefits."]]},{"title":"Working while collecting benefits: 2026 retirement earnings test","body":["The Social Security retirement earnings test can withhold benefits from someone receiving retirement benefits before full retirement age and earning above specified limits from work. For 2026, the general annual earnings limit is $24,480 for a recipient who remains under full retirement age all year, and SSA withholds $1 of benefits for each $2 of earnings above the limit, subject to actual payment mechanics. In the year the worker reaches FRA, the applicable higher 2026 limit is $65,160 for earnings in months before FRA and the withholding rule is $1 per $3 above that amount.","SSA does not generally count investment dividends, interest, pensions or annuity income as work earnings for this particular test, even though those items can be taxable and affect other household planning. At or after the month full retirement age is reached, the retirement earnings test stops reducing benefits. SSA later recalculates benefits to credit months in which amounts were reduced or withheld, so the withholding is not always equivalent to a permanently lost pension stream.","Imagine an under-FRA worker in 2026 earning $33,400 in wages with a $9,600 annual gross retirement benefit. Earnings exceed the $24,480 threshold by $8,920, producing $4,460 test withholding; gross benefits actually paid could be roughly $5,140 under the simplified annual illustration. Special first-year monthly rules, payment administration and recalculation can change the exact real case."],"worked":["2026 under-FRA annual earned wages $33,400.","Excess wages = $33,400−$24,480 = $8,920.","Required simplified benefit withholding = $8,920/2=$4,460.","Annual entitled benefit $9,600 ⇒ remaining gross cash received ≈$5,140 for that year's example."],"practice":[["Do retirement account dividends count the same as wages toward the SSA earnings test?","Generally no. The earnings test focuses on wages and net self-employment earnings, although investment income can affect tax and means-tested programs."],["Is the full $4,460 necessarily gone forever?","Not necessarily. SSA can recompute benefits at full retirement age to credit months of benefits withheld, subject to applicable rules."]]},{"title":"Taxation of Social Security has its own definition of provisional income","body":["Federal taxable Social Security is determined with a provisional or combined-income method that generally adds half of the Social Security benefits to other relevant income, including certain tax-exempt interest. For single filers, common lower and upper base amounts are $25,000 and $34,000; for married joint filers, $32,000 and $44,000. Depending on the calculation, up to 50% or up to 85% of benefits can become includible in federal taxable income. This does not mean Social Security is subject to a flat 85% tax rate.","Consider an unmarried worker with $22,000 annual Social Security and $20,000 of other relevant income without special exclusions. Combined income is $20,000 +0.5×$22,000=$31,000. In a simplified first-tier case, $6,000 above the $25,000 lower threshold can make up to $3,000 (50% of that excess, subject to the allowable cap) of Social Security taxable. The amount of tax on the resulting income depends separately on deductions and marginal income-tax brackets.","Traditional IRA withdrawals or Roth conversions can increase other income and cause a larger fraction of benefits to become taxable; qualified Roth distributions generally have different effects. Tax-aware withdrawal sequencing can reduce avoidable interactions, but a complete return needs the IRS Publication 915 worksheets, eligibility and current-year law. State treatment is not identical."],"worked":["Annual Social Security benefit $22,000; other relevant income $20,000.","Combined income $20,000+$11,000=$31,000.","Illustrative first-tier taxable Social Security amount min($11,000, 0.5×($31,000−$25,000)) = $3,000.","The taxpayer's income-tax liability on the resulting taxable-income base must be calculated separately."],"practice":[["Does '85% of Social Security taxable' mean an 85% tax rate?","No. It means as much as 85% of benefit dollars may be included in taxable income, which is then taxed using applicable income-tax rates."],["Why can a traditional retirement distribution affect taxation of Social Security?","It can increase other relevant income within the provisional-income worksheet, causing a larger share of Social Security benefits to enter federal taxable income."]]},{"title":"Connecticut Social Security deductions are different from federal taxation","body":["Connecticut's retirement income treatment can be comparatively favorable for eligible beneficiaries while retaining its own income limits. DRS guidance says Social Security benefits included in federal adjusted gross income can be fully exempt from Connecticut tax when federal AGI is below $75,000 for single or married separate filers, and below $100,000 for joint filers and heads of household. Individuals above those thresholds may still qualify for a partial subtraction with statutory calculation, rather than a simplistic complete loss of state benefit.","A fictitious married household with $38,000 Social Security and federal adjusted gross income of $70,000 can be eligible to subtract the federal taxable portion for Connecticut purposes under that threshold illustration. That does not revise the federal Form 1040 tax on Social Security. The state-specific subtraction is added in its own modified-AGI computation, and actual rules, filing status and state schedules control.","Retirement tax planning must separate three cash-flow layers: Social Security cash received, federal taxable portion and Connecticut taxable portion. A claim that 'Social Security is tax free in Connecticut' without income qualifications or federal distinctions can mislead homeowners, particularly when Roth conversions or RMDs increase AGI. Use the official DRS Social Security benefit adjustment calculator before final filing."],"worked":["Hypothetical MFJ federal AGI $70,000 with qualifying federally taxable Social Security included.","Since $70,000 is below CT's cited $100,000 MFJ threshold, the federal taxable Social Security component can qualify for full CT subtraction under current rules.","This Connecticut deduction does not remove federal Social Security tax.","At higher AGI, the state adjustment must be calculated under state worksheets rather than assumed zero."],"practice":[["Does a Connecticut Social Security subtraction necessarily eliminate federal income tax on benefits?","No. Federal and Connecticut income tax calculations are separate; the subtraction affects the Connecticut return, not the federal base."],["Why should Roth conversion size be coordinated with Connecticut's retirement-income rules?","The conversion can increase federal AGI and alter state exemptions or phaseouts, changing the combined after-tax outcome."]]},{"title":"Medicare, survivor income and the retirement-income floor","body":["Social Security claiming should not be confused with Medicare enrollment. Most people become eligible for Medicare around age 65 regardless of whether they delay Social Security until 70; missing Medicare enrollment windows can generate penalties or coverage gaps without an applicable exception. Medicare premiums, Part B and Part D income-related adjustments, private coverage, and out-of-pocket healthcare expenses affect the retirement spending budget. A larger gross Social Security check is not the same as the same increase in spendable cash.","Survivor benefits, spousal benefits, pension survivor options and longevity insurance shape household security. One spouse may outlive the other by decades; the survivor often loses one benefit stream but retains much of the household's housing and healthcare cost. A retirement model should therefore include both spouses, benefit election rights, age differences, disability and survivor income rather than merely projecting one portfolio balance.","Separately build a baseline retirement income floor from Social Security and guaranteed pension amounts, and a flexible draw from investments for discretionary purchases. Stress healthcare inflation, unexpected home repair and long-term care. This is not an instruction to buy an annuity: all guarantees depend on contractual terms, issuer strength, inflation provisions, fees and suitability."],"worked":["Hypothetical household essential spending $3,400/month.","Social Security $2,200/month plus dependable pension $700/month ⇒ floor $2,900/month before taxes/premiums.","Essential gap before taxes/healthcare = $500/month, or $6,000/year.","A widow(er) scenario might remove one income source while retaining much of spending; recompute rather than assuming household needs halve."],"practice":[["Why is SSA's $2,200 benefit not necessarily $2,200 spendable cash?","Federal taxes, state treatment, Medicare premiums, healthcare charges and household cash-flow constraints can reduce net spendable funds."],["Why can survivor benefits change the value of waiting to claim?","The higher earner's delayed benefit can alter what a survivor may receive, making the decision a household lifetime insurance choice."]]},{"title":"Integrate personal savings, Social Security and an evidence-based claiming memo","body":["A professional retirement income memorandum starts with a documented SSA benefit statement for each eligible person, a realistic end-of-work date, current savings, expected contributions, pension rights, full retirement age, a credible mortality range, and estimated taxes. Compare claim ages 62, FRA and 70 using gross annual payments, present values and survivor scenarios. Identify years in which retirement savings must bridge the income gap.","The analysis should model claiming alternatives under pessimistic, central and optimistic investment returns and the same inflation assumptions. Social Security COLAs help preserve purchasing power under law, while fixed nominal pensions may lose real value. Real versus nominal model consistency is crucial: a 3% future price increase is not an after-tax investment return. The analyst should show uncertainty rather than choose a retirement age using just one break-even number.","Financial planning ethics require distinguishing government benefit estimates from guaranteed personal benefit outcomes, treating benefit taxation accurately and acknowledging legislative and longevity risk. A defensible memo can conclude that a smaller benefit now is best if the household faces immediate income needs or severe health risks; it can equally justify delayed claiming when bridge assets and survivor protection support that choice."],"worked":["For a fictional 1960+ birth cohort worker, compare $1,400 at age 62, $2,000 at FRA 67 and $2,480 at age 70.","Compute 8-year gross catch-up from age 62 to 70 = $134,400; subsequent monthly advantage $1,080.","Compare lifetime gross receipts at death ages 75, 80, 85 and 95 plus discounted present values.","State assumptions for Medicare premiums, withholding, spouse, inflation and actual SSA earnings history."],"practice":[["Why should a claim-age analysis display more than one longevity horizon?","Benefit timing trades earlier income against later larger income, so the winner depends on lifespan and the ability to finance the waiting period."],["What is missing from a 'largest lifetime gross sum' decision?","Discount rates, tax, healthcare, survivor protections, inflation, household liquidity, personal health and the risk of running out of savings."]]}],"sourceRefs":[["SSA official 2026 Social Security cost-of-living and earnings facts","https://www.ssa.gov/cola/factsheets/2026.html"],["SSA 2026 full retirement age and benefit information","https://www.ssa.gov/policy/docs/chartbooks/fast_facts/2026/fast_facts26.html"],["SSA benefits for individuals born in 1960 or later","https://www.ssa.gov/benefits/retirement/planner/1960.html"],["SSA delayed claiming percentages for 1960+ workers","https://www.ssa.gov/benefits/retirement/planner/1960-delay.html"],["SSA retirement earnings test and 2026 rules","https://www.ssa.gov/benefits/retirement/planner/whileworking.html"],["SSA lifetime earnings and retirement estimates","https://www.ssa.gov/retirement"],["IRS Publication 915 taxability of Social Security","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p915"],["Connecticut DRS guidance for retirees and Social Security","https://portal.ct.gov/drs/individuals/connecticut-tax-tips-for-senior-citizens"],["Medicare timing and initial enrollment","https://www.medicare.gov/basics/get-started-with-medicare/sign-up/when-can-i-sign-up-for-medicare"]],"assessment":{"title":"Social Security statement reconstruction and claiming committee","prompt":"Assume a hypothetical person born in 1960 or later has $2,000/month primary insurance amount at full retirement age 67. Compute checks at 62, 67 and 70 using SSA standard approximate percentages; annual cash flows, cumulative benefits at ages 75/80/85/95, and gross age-62-versus-age-70 break-even. Compute the 2026 earnings test for an under-FRA worker with $33,400 earnings and $9,600 gross benefit. Separately compute simplified first-tier Social Security taxable portion for single taxpayer with $22,000 benefits and $20,000 other income. Provide an 800–1,200-word household recommendation explaining COLA, mortality, taxes, survivor and Medicare considerations and verified SSA evidence.","rubric":["PIA, benefit percentages and cohort assumptions (25%)","Cumulative and present-value logic with caveats (20%)","2026 earnings test and FRA recalculation explanation (15%)","Social Security provisional-income taxation and Connecticut adjustment (20%)","Medicare, survivor and professional judgment (20%)"],"answerKey":["For FRA 67 / PIA $2,000: approximately $1,400 at 62, $2,000 at 67, $2,480 at 70.","Gross annual benefits $16,800/$24,000/$29,760 when receiving a full 12 months.","62 vs 70 catch-up: 96 months×$1,400=$134,400 early benefits; monthly later advantage $1,080; break-even 124.444 months after age 70, approximately age 80.37.","2026 earnings test: ($33,400−$24,480)/2=$4,460, with $5,140 of annual $9,600 gross benefits remaining in the simplified illustration.","Single combined income $20,000+$11,000=$31,000; simplified first-tier taxable benefit $3,000, subject to exact worksheet.","Strong analysis distinguishes gross receipts from present value, federal tax, CT tax and Medicare/survivor considerations."]}},{"id":"L12.S4","title":"Sequence Risk and Withdrawals","subtitle":"Retirement withdrawal mathematics, risk capacity, RMDs, decumulation taxes and household survival","prerequisites":["L7.S3 time value of money, annuities and inflation","L10.S1 portfolio risk and goal buckets","L10.S4 investment policy and governance","L11.S3 tax character and gains","L12.S2 retirement account treatment","L12.S3 Social Security and retirement cash flows"],"objectives":["Calculate sustainable-needs funding gaps and initial withdrawal percentages without treating them as guarantees","Demonstrate path dependency and sequence-of-returns effects with equal nominal investment returns","Model fixed-real, percentage-of-portfolio and guardrail spending approaches","Build nominal versus real annual cash-flow tables with healthcare/repair stresses","Apply simplified RMD formula, owner and inherited-account restrictions and tax effects","Evaluate traditional/Roth/taxable withdrawal order with multiple tax sources","Build a defensible retirement spending IPS with liquidity, rebalancing and estate governance"],"sections":[{"title":"Retirement changes the direction of the cash-flow system","body":["Before retirement, labor income often funds contributions and the investment portfolio grows without planned withdrawals. After retirement, assets must fund spending while still absorbing market risk, inflation, fees and taxes. This transition is called decumulation. A household's net worth can remain high while it is unable to pay a new roof repair, healthcare bill or property-tax increase without selling depreciated securities. Portfolio design now begins with dated spending liabilities and an income floor.","Start with essential spending after tax, discretionary spending, expected Social Security, pensions, annuity income, mortgage costs and healthcare. A spendable income gap must be financed by withdrawals or other work. A simplistic retirement target such as 'ten times salary' may mislead a homeowner with large housing maintenance, dependents or unusually high medical costs. Express the requirement in annual real purchasing-power units and map when benefits actually begin.","Households also face longevity risk: nobody knows the year of last withdrawal. The goal is not to compute a single terminal account balance at age 90 but to keep a feasible household plan through favorable, average and severe outcomes. Scenario analysis cannot guarantee a minimum return, and a probabilistic success rate is meaningful only when the return, inflation, longevity and tax assumptions are disclosed."],"worked":["Essential annual expenses $42,000; income floor from Social Security and pensions $30,000.","Initial essential spending gap $12,000/year before state/federal taxes or other omitted expenses.","If discretionary spending $6,000 is desired, total $18,000 annual withdrawal need before tax adjustments.","Compare with genuinely withdrawable portfolio value, protected cash and lifespan scenarios."],"practice":[["Does a retirement portfolio with large net worth guarantee next month's mortgage payment?","No. Tax, volatility, liquidity, restrictions and timing can prevent economical access to sufficient cash when it is needed."],["Why should essential income shortfall be separated from discretionary spending?","Essentials need more reliable financing, while flexible discretionary spending can respond to markets, taxes and unexpected costs."]]},{"title":"Withdrawal rates are initial spending rules, not investment returns","body":["The initial withdrawal rate equals first-year gross withdrawals divided by the portfolio value at the start of retirement. A $24,000 first-year withdrawal from a $600,000 portfolio is 4%. The historical '4% rule' is usually discussed as an initial withdrawal followed by inflation-adjusted dollar increases, not a recommendation to withdraw exactly 4% of the remaining account every year. The two methods are mathematically different under a market decline.","An inflation-adjusted fixed-real policy can create stable spending if assets support it, but fixed spending raises portfolio stress when markets fall. A constant percentage-of-current-portfolio policy reduces withdrawals after losses and increases them after gains, offering greater portfolio flexibility at the expense of volatile household income. A guardrail policy allows controlled spending adjustments when portfolio funded status changes, but requires written thresholds and a workable minimum spending floor.","A research-based plan tests horizon, asset allocation, taxes, fees and health-cost uncertainty before selecting a number. Historic withdrawal-rate studies rely on particular markets and assumptions, and cannot guarantee future real returns. For a 45-year retirement horizon, high inflation or a low-return regime, a 4% fixed-real starting rate may not be appropriate."],"worked":["Opening portfolio $600,000; first-year gross withdrawal $24,000 ⇒ 4.00% initial rate.","If portfolio declines to $450,000, a constant 4% current-balance policy yields $18,000/year.","An unchanged $24,000 nominal dollar policy would now withdraw 5.33% of $450,000 before inflation.","If fixed-real spending rises 3% for inflation, next-year target becomes $24,720, or 5.49% of the reduced $450,000."],"practice":[["Is the historical 4% heuristic a guarantee of 30 years of retirement spending?","No. It depends on investment markets, portfolio mix, inflation, expenses and horizon, and does not promise a specific survival probability."],["What is the trade-off of withdrawing a constant percentage of current assets?","It mechanically scales spending to portfolio value, reducing pressure after losses but causing potentially large drops in household income."]]},{"title":"Sequence risk: identical average returns can produce different cash outcomes","body":["Investment returns multiply account balances, but withdrawals subtract a fixed number of dollars between market periods. When a negative return arrives early, more shares must be sold to generate the same spending, leaving less capital to participate in recovery. With no withdrawals, two annual returns of −30% and +30% produce the same ending balance regardless of order: $100,000×0.7×1.3=$91,000. The product is the same. With a withdrawal between those returns, the end balances diverge.","Apply a $10,000 withdrawal after the first year's investment result. If the portfolio falls 30% first, $100,000 becomes $70,000; withdraw $10,000 to $60,000; then a +30% return ends at $78,000. If the market rises 30% first, $100,000 becomes $130,000; withdraw $10,000 to $120,000; then a −30% return ends at $84,000. The same returns, same withdrawal and same two-year arithmetic average create a $6,000 difference.","This effect is why retirement income planning cannot be evaluated using only a smooth annual average expected return. An investment with 6% estimated average return does not deliver a predictable 6% cash interest payment. Early drawdowns, inflation and large mandatory expenses can interact destructively. The appropriate stress test applies realistic sequences rather than a single deterministic growth curve."],"worked":["Loss-first: $100,000×0.70−$10,000=$60,000; ×1.30=$78,000.","Gain-first: $100,000×1.30−$10,000=$120,000; ×0.70=$84,000.","Difference between ending values = $6,000, despite identical return observations.","With no intermediate withdrawal, both sequences end at $91,000 (100k×0.7×1.3)."],"practice":[["Why do equal arithmetic-average returns not imply equal retirement outcomes?","The timing of returns relative to withdrawals changes the number of shares remaining to participate in subsequent gains and losses."],["Does the example prove one market sequence is always better without considering withdrawals?","No. In this simplified multiplicative model without withdrawals, reversing the return order produces the same final value. Withdrawals introduce path dependency."]]},{"title":"Inflation is a liability shock that compounds in retirement","body":["An inflation-adjusted spending plan specifies whether a dollar amount is expressed in today's purchasing power or future nominal dollars. If a household needs $24,000 in spending in year one and annual inflation is a hypothetical steady 3%, the nominal amount required after two annual increases is $24,000×1.03²=$25,461.60. This increase is not investment profit; it is the higher number of nominal dollars required for similar purchasing power.","Healthcare, insurance premiums, property taxes and home repairs can rise at rates different from general consumer inflation. A homeowner living in Connecticut may have a fixed-rate mortgage payment, yet insurance, municipal property tax, utilities and maintenance are not fixed by that mortgage. A blanket 3% assumption can conceal significant spending dispersion. Model separate categories and avoid replacing actual local tax/insurance quotes with national inflation averages.","High unexpected inflation can be especially damaging with long-duration nominal bonds, cash balances and fixed pensions. Treasury inflation-protected securities and indexed Social Security can help with some inflation exposures but have their own price, tax and timing risks. Explicitly compare real returns, nominal returns and cash-flow needs in consistent units."],"worked":["Annual spending requirement $24,000; annual inflation assumed 3%.","Year 2 nominal requirement $24,720.","Year 3 nominal requirement $25,461.60.","At 6% nominal investment return and 3% inflation, exact real return =1.06/1.03−1≈2.9126%, before fees and taxes."],"practice":[["Is a 6% nominal investment gain the same as a 6% increase in purchasing power with 3% inflation?","No. The real purchasing-power return is approximately 2.91% using (1.06/1.03)−1, before taxes and fees."],["Why should homeowners stress property taxes separately from mortgage principal and interest?","A fixed-rate mortgage may stabilize debt service while taxes, insurance and maintenance expenses still rise or fluctuate."]]},{"title":"Liquidity buckets, bond ladders and the danger of false certainty","body":["A retirement cash-flow policy may reserve one or more years of near-term withdrawals in liquid deposits or short-dated high-quality bonds, while keeping a diversified long-horizon portfolio. This goal-matching approach can reduce the need to sell equities during a downturn, but buckets do not create higher market returns or remove inflation and rollover risk. Every bucket must have a refill rule, estimated taxes and an audit trail.","Suppose essential net portfolio withdrawals are estimated at $12,000 annually. A 24-month withdrawal reserve requires $24,000 before allowing for taxes, unexpected repairs and investment return on the reserve. That $24,000 is already part of household assets, not a new bonus in addition to the brokerage balance. A ladder of individual bonds can mature on spending dates, but issuer credit, reinvestment and interest-rate valuation risk still matter, particularly if bonds are sold before maturity.","During a downturn, a retiree could use earmarked cash to meet expenses while allowing diversified stocks time to recover; no recovery timetable is guaranteed. An emergency rule should specify when spending is reduced, how the reserve is replenished, and what happens if the market does not rebound for five years. Claims that the bucket method eliminates sequence risk are overstated."],"worked":["Annual essential portfolio withdrawal requirement $12,000.","Two-year protected withdrawal reserve = 2×$12,000=$24,000, before tax and repair buffers.","A $30,000 emergency and withdrawal reserve is not $30,000 extra net worth beyond the recorded accounts.","An additional $8,000 roof repair stress reduces accessible cash and forces a refill/spending decision."],"practice":[["Why should a retirement cash bucket not be counted again as extra wealth?","It is simply a designated portion of existing assets, and counting it separately from portfolio value double counts the same dollars."],["Can a ladder of maturing bonds guarantee no loss under all conditions?","No. Credit default, inflation, issuer provisions, liquidity needs before maturity and reinvestment can still create risk."]]},{"title":"Required minimum distributions: tax recognition on a statutory schedule","body":["Required minimum distributions (RMDs) compel minimum withdrawals from many pretax IRAs and employer retirement plans under age- and beneficiary-based rules. The starting age depends on birth year under SECURE 2.0, so a 2026 calculator must not assume every future retiree begins at exactly 73. For a typical owner at age 73 subject to the Uniform Lifetime Table, the IRS denominator is 26.5. The generic RMD is prior December 31 account balance divided by the applicable life expectancy denominator; another table can apply if a spouse who is the sole beneficiary is more than ten years younger.","With a prior-year traditional IRA balance of $400,000 and Uniform Lifetime Table divisor 26.5, a simplified required minimum distribution is $15,094.34. The RMD is not an added investment return: it transfers money out of a tax-deferred wrapper and generally increases taxable ordinary income to the extent the account was untaxed. The owner can reinvest the after-tax proceeds in a brokerage account if other rules permit, but cannot simply roll over the RMD back into a tax-deferred plan.","The first distribution may be delayed to April 1 of the year after the first RMD year where law permits, but that can require taking two taxable distributions in one calendar year. An employer-plan still-working exception may apply to some participants but not to traditional IRAs or certain owners. Roth IRAs and designated Roth accounts generally have no lifetime RMDs for the original owner, while inherited Roth accounts can still require distributions."],"worked":["Previous December 31 traditional IRA balance $400,000.","Age 73 Uniform Lifetime divisor 26.5.","RMD ≈$400,000/26.5=$15,094.34 for the year in this qualifying example.","At a hypothetical stable 22% federal marginal rate, isolated tax effect on fully pretax withdrawal ≈$3,320.75, not a final tax return."],"practice":[["Is the annual RMD calculated by simply taking a fixed 4% of all traditional IRAs?","No. It uses the prior year-end value and an IRS life expectancy denominator, which varies by age and some spouse circumstances."],["If a retiree spends only $5,000 but is required to distribute $15,094, can the remainder stay inside the same IRA as an unwithdrawn RMD?","No. The required amount must be distributed. After any applicable tax, unused cash may be invested elsewhere subject to normal rules."]]},{"title":"Distribution order is a tax modeling problem, not a universal recipe","body":["Retirees may have taxable brokerage assets with basis and gains, fully pretax IRA money, Roth contributions/earnings, pensions and Social Security. The mix determines tax character and potential provisional income, preferential capital gain rates and Medicare income-related premium thresholds. A fixed order such as 'always empty taxable accounts first' can create unnecessary future RMD pressure. But the reverse claim 'always convert the entire traditional account to Roth immediately' can push the household into high current brackets.","An effective annual model starts with cash required after tax, maps required RMDs and Social Security, and compares discretionary withdrawals from available account sources. It estimates ordinary income, capital gains, qualified dividends, state modifications and estimated tax. It then checks Medicare premium effects and public-benefit eligibility as relevant. Because one federal tax dollar can alter another tax calculation, simplistic add-two-marginal-rates arithmetic may be unreliable.","Roth balances can be valuable as flexible qualified-distribution reserves, particularly to fund an expensive home repair without increasing taxable ordinary income. They are not costless: Roth contributions and conversions consumed after-tax savings in prior years. The plan must consider heirs and the opportunity cost of reducing protected Roth assets."],"worked":["Retirement essentials require $42,000/year; pensions/SSA gross cash $30,000/year.","Gross cash gap before tax adjustments $12,000, but taxable withdrawals may need to exceed $12,000 to deliver that net amount.","A $12,000 fully pretax IRA distribution at stable illustrative 12% tax rate leaves $10,560 after federal tax alone.","A qualified Roth distribution of $12,000 may provide $12,000 cash without the same federal ordinary tax, but prior after-tax funding costs remain."],"practice":[["Why is a gross $12,000 traditional IRA withdrawal not necessarily $12,000 of spendable income?","Taxable portions usually create federal and potentially state income tax, and other income-tested calculations can change."],["Why is it unsafe to claim Roth distributions are universally the best first withdrawal source?","Doing so can consume scarce tax-free flexibility unnecessarily, while realizing taxable income in low bracket years or taking required distributions could be better across a lifetime."]]},{"title":"Inherited retirement accounts and the final decade risk","body":["Retirement accounts often transfer according to beneficiary designations rather than solely through probate documents. For most non-eligible designated beneficiaries after the owner's death, a ten-year full-distribution rule applies under the SECURE Act, although annual minimum payments can be required when death occurs after the required beginning date. Surviving spouses, eligible designated beneficiaries, minors who are children of the decedent and certain disabled or chronically ill beneficiaries can have distinct rules. A trust beneficiary can complicate rather than simplify the distribution schedule.","An heir who receives a large pretax IRA can face significant ordinary income tax when distributions are compressed into a short period. A Roth IRA beneficiary may often receive tax-free qualified distributions but is not exempt from all payout deadlines. Estate-tax inclusion, income-tax basis, life insurance and direct versus trust beneficiary decisions require independent legal review. Inherited IRA management is not the same as simply placing the balance in a joint brokerage account.","A professional household plan includes incapacity, survivor spending, a contact list of custodians, beneficiary designation confirmations and tax return preparation logistics. The academically defensible answer sometimes is that the person needs an estate attorney and enrolled agent or CPA; no investment calculator substitutes for those decisions."],"worked":["Inherited pretax IRA $150,000 with a ten-year full payout requirement in a simplified scenario.","Equal nominal principal-only payouts would average $15,000/year, but actual year-end distributions, investment changes and required annual amounts vary.","A $150,000 one-year distribution can be taxed very differently from spreading withdrawals across allowed years.","Verify beneficiary type and death-year RMD status before using the ten-year illustration; no automatic universal ten-year equal-payout rule."],"practice":[["Does the ten-year inherited IRA rule mean every beneficiary may wait until the final day of year ten to distribute everything?","No. Some beneficiary classes and post-required-beginning-date deaths can create annual minimum distribution obligations, and other special rules apply."],["Can designating a trust automatically avoid income tax on pretax retirement distributions?","No. Trust terms and beneficiary classification can change payout taxation and timing, sometimes adversely; professional estate/tax coordination is needed."]]},{"title":"A multi-year retirement policy and stress-test committee","body":["The cumulative decumulation project starts with essential and discretionary annual spending, household longevity, Social Security start dates, property tax and insurance escalation, current investment accounts and restrictions, and planned beneficiary transfers. Separate the nominal and real timeline. Calculate the initial withdrawal rate, tax effects and a minimum liquid reserve, then run a sequence-of-returns crisis where stocks fall 35%, bonds fall 5%, inflation reaches 5% and a $10,000 home repair occurs.","A 60/40 retirement portfolio of $200,000 exposed to stocks falling 35% and bonds falling 5% declines to $200,000×[0.6×0.65+0.4×0.95]=$154,000 before fees and withdrawals. If a planned $8,000 gross withdrawal follows, the portfolio falls to $146,000, and the next year's $8,000 spending is over 5.48% of that new balance even without inflation. Add a 5% inflation adjustment and a $10,000 roof repair, and the household must decide whether to reduce discretionary spending, use reserves or alter asset sales.","The final policy names spending guardrails, liquidity floor, allocation targets, annual tax review, RMD deadlines, surviving-spouse continuity and exception approvals. It should be auditable by another analyst and usable by a partner who did not write it. The objective is a resilient plan under adverse conditions, not a claim that any withdrawal method guarantees retirement success."],"worked":["60% of $200,000 stocks = $120,000, losing 35% ⇒ $78,000.","40% bonds = $80,000 losing 5% ⇒ $76,000.","Portfolio post-shock $78,000+$76,000=$154,000; after $8,000 planned withdrawal $146,000.","Without inflation, $8,000/$146,000≈5.48%; with 5% annual spending increase $8,400/$146,000≈5.75%, before extra home repair."],"practice":[["Why is 4% of the starting $200,000 not still 4% of the portfolio after a market crash?","The withdrawal amount stays $8,000 but the denominator falls; the withdrawal rate on remaining assets rises substantially, increasing stress."],["What makes a retirement investment policy operational rather than merely theoretical?","Documented cash floors, withdrawal order, taxes, RMD dates, stress triggers, spending cuts, rebalancing conditions, beneficiary procedures and responsible review roles."]]}],"sourceRefs":[["IRS RMD rules and age-based denominators","https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-participant-employee/retirement-topics-required-minimum-distributions-rmds"],["IRS 2025 Publication 590-B with 2026 RMD examples and Uniform Lifetime Table","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p590b"],["IRS retirement plan and IRA RMD FAQs","https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/retirement-plan-and-ira-required-minimum-distributions-faqs"],["IRS beneficiary rules","https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/plan-participant-employee/retirement-topics-beneficiary"],["IRS Publication 915 federal Social Security income treatment","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p915"],["IRS 2026 retirement contribution limit explanation","https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/cola-increases-for-dollar-limitations-on-benefits-and-contributions"],["Investor.gov risk and allocation basics","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/getting-started/asset-allocation"],["Connecticut DRS retirement income taxation and subtraction","https://portal.ct.gov/drs/individuals/connecticut-tax-tips-for-senior-citizens"],["Social Security full retirement and delayed benefits","https://www.ssa.gov/retirement"]],"assessment":{"title":"Cumulative retirement income stress-test and decumulation investment policy","prompt":"Write an investment-policy and tax-source memorandum for a Connecticut couple with a $200,000 60/40 taxable retirement portfolio; protected separate emergency fund $6,000; essential living expenses $42,000; annual gross Social Security and pension income $30,000; planned pre-tax $12,000 withdrawal gap; and a separate 73-year-old's $400,000 traditional IRA (Uniform Lifetime denominator 26.5). Compute an initial $8,000 4%-of-$200k test, stock loss 35%, bond loss 5%, $8,000 withdrawal after shock, and a 5% inflation adjusted following spending level. Calculate RMD from the IRA, tax at an assumed stable 22% federal ordinary rate, and alternatives for taking required cash. Compare loss-first and gain-first $100k portfolio paths with $10k intermediate withdrawals. Recommend a cash-reserve, guardrail and taxable/traditional/Roth draw policy, including Connecticut tax, survivor risks and beneficiary deadlines, with at least three adverse scenarios.","rubric":["Portfolio shock and sequence-return calculations (25%)","RMD and distribution tax calculations with limitations (20%)","Initial versus flexible withdrawal and inflation modeling (20%)","Household liquidity and survivor/inheritance design (20%)","Source quality, reporting limits, and stress-tested professional judgment (15%)"],"answerKey":["$200,000×[0.60×0.65+0.40×0.95]=$154,000 after market shock; after $8,000 withdrawal $146,000.","Initial $8,000/$200,000=4.00%; after shock and draw, next $8,000/$146,000≈5.479%; 5% higher next withdrawal $8,400/$146,000≈5.753%.","RMD $400,000/26.5=$15,094.3396; illustrative 22% federal tax effect ≈$3,320.75 when fully pretax, before all other taxes/interactions.","Loss-first $78,000 and gain-first $84,000; no intermediate draw both $91,000.","Essential annual gap before tax $42,000−$30,000=$12,000; must gross up for tax and consider timing and restricted cash.","The written policy recognizes one-time repairs, income uncertainty, state subtractions, Medicare, inherited IRA RMDs, and does not guarantee a particular safe withdrawal rate."]}}],"capstone":{"title":"Module 12 — Retirement planning, Social Security and tax committee","intro":"Four independent households in one integrated advisory file. Each exhibits a different retirement life stage. Use 2026 law where indicated; returns, tax rates, income and prices are hypothetical. Do not combine the retiree's IRA with the worker's budget unless case facts explicitly say they are the same household.","scenario":["Worker A in Connecticut is age 42 with $60,000 eligible annual pay and a current $250/month possible retirement budget while protecting $6,000 emergency liquidity and a future home fund.","Worker A plan matches 50% of the first 6% of eligible pay; plan has 24 equal paychecks, per-pay-period matching and NO annual true-up. Employer source $8,000 is 40% vested; employee source $12,000 is always vested.","Worker B can set aside exactly $3,000 after current federal taxes for retirement today. Current federal marginal rate is assumed stable 22%; assumed constant return 6% a year over 20 years; future tax rates tested at 12%,22%,32%. Roth distributions are qualified and traditional contributions fully deductible.","Worker B also owns traditional/SEP/SIMPLE IRAs with aggregated modeled value $100,000 and $10,000 nondeductible basis; an eligible $10,000 conversion occurs in the same tax year under a simplified Form 8606 pro-rata model.","Worker C was born in 1960 or later, has illustrative full-retirement-age benefit $2,000/month at age 67, and considers claiming at 62, 67 or 70. Each benefit is assumed to stay at 70%, 100%, 124% of PIA in the nominal gross demonstration.","Worker C is under full retirement age for all of 2026, has wages $33,400 and gross annual benefit $9,600 for the SSA earnings-test exercise. The earnings-test illustration is independent of the age-claim values.","Worker D owns a $200,000 60% equity/40% bond portfolio and must take $8,000 before-tax annual withdrawal. Separate owned traditional IRA $400,000 has Uniform Lifetime divisor 26.5 for a qualified 73-year-old owner.","Model D's first-year stocks −35%, bonds −5%, then the $8,000 withdrawal, plus 5% increase in next year's nominal $8,000 planned withdrawal.","Household retirement needs include $42,000 annual essentials and a hypothetical $30,000 Social Security/pension income floor; taxation may require an additional gross-up.","The committee must assess possible CT state income adjustments and inheritance options, using official tax-year sources rather than tax-shelter marketing claims."],"tasks":["Define tax year and assumptions for every limit and income-tax rate used.","Worker A: calculate $2,500 per paycheck, 6% employee deferral and payroll match for the 24 periods.","Worker A: compare matches on 4%,5%,6%,10% eligible payroll deferrals.","Worker A: calculate the employer match lost with six unqualified paychecks and no annual true-up.","Worker A: compute vested versus potentially forfeitable balances and document vesting years.","Worker A: compare full match contribution requirement with $250/month actual liquidity.","Worker B: compute equal-after-tax-cost pretax and Roth contributions.","Worker B: compound both 20 years at 6% and show future taxes at 12%,22%,32%.","Worker B: explain 2026 combined IRA limits and eligibility caveats.","Worker B: calculate the nondeductible basis ratio and taxable Roth conversion using Form 8606 intuition.","Worker B: state the Roth IRA five-year/ordering risks and prohibited shortcuts.","Worker C: compute age 62/67/70 monthly and annual benefit alternatives.","Worker C: calculate nominal break-even of claims at 62 versus 70 and critique the assumption.","Worker C: compute 2026 SSA earnings-test withholding on $33,400 wages.","Worker C: show why federal taxable Social Security and Connecticut state adjustment are different calculations.","Worker D: compute the 60/40 portfolio shock, planned withdrawal and inflation-raised next withdrawal percentage.","Worker D: compute the 73-year-old's 2026 RMD from $400,000/26.5 and isolated 22% income-tax sensitivity.","Worker D: compute ±30% returns with a $10,000 intermediate withdrawal and explain sequence risk.","Document household cash reserves, permitted and prohibited asset uses, beneficiaries and Medicare enrollment risks.","Deliver a 1,500–2,000 word retirement advisory memo including tax-aware draw strategy, alternative scenarios, ethical constraints and cited official sources."],"answers":["For 2026 standard 401(k) deferral cap $24,500; catch-up $8,000 at 50+ or $11,250 at 60–63 if eligible; IRA limit $7,500 under 50, $8,600 at 50+.","A: $60,000/24=$2,500 paycheck; 6%= $150 employee/paycheck; 50% match $75/paycheck; annual $3,600 employee, $1,800 employer.","A: matches on 4/5/6/10% employee deferrals are $1,200/$1,500/$1,800/$1,800 respectively.","A: 18 matched periods ×$75=$1,350; missed match $450 versus 24 fully qualified paychecks.","A: employee $12,000 vested; employer $8,000×40%=$3,200 vested; total $15,200; unvested $4,800.","A: six-percent employee contribution equals $300/month, which is $50 more than $250/month available. A full-match plan cannot be assumed funded.","B: gross pretax contribution for a $3,000 after-tax cost at 22% = $3,846.153846; Roth $3,000.","B: 1.06^20≈3.20713547; Roth ≈$9,621.41. Traditional pre-tax balance ≈$12,335.14; after 12/22/32% tax ≈$10,854.92/$9,621.41/$8,387.90.","B: 2026 IRA $7,500 combined traditional/Roth under 50; compensation and modified-AGI limits apply independently.","B: 10% nondeductible basis / $100,000 aggregate; $10,000 conversion => $1,000 nontaxable, $9,000 taxable under simplified example.","B: Roth IRA regular contribution ordering differs from Roth 401(k) nonqualified pro-rata distributions, and conversion-specific five-year risks may apply.","C: $1,400/$2,000/$2,480 per month for claims at 62/67/70; annual $16,800/$24,000/$29,760.","C: earlier 62 start creates $134,400 received by age 70. Later monthly advantage $1,080 => break-even 124.444 months after age 70 (~80.37 years) without discounting, COLA or survivor differences.","C: SSA 2026 earnings test ($33,400−$24,480)/2=$4,460 withheld, assuming under-FRA all year; $5,140 of annual $9,600 payable before payment mechanics.","C: federal taxable Social Security uses combined-income formulas, while qualifying CT taxpayers can subtract state-taxable portions under income-sensitive CT rules.","D: stocks $120,000×0.65=$78,000; bonds $80,000×0.95=$76,000; post-shock $154,000; after $8,000 draw $146,000.","D: initial draw 4% of $200k; $8,000/$146k ≈5.479%, or inflation-adjusted $8,400/$146k≈5.753%.","D: RMD $400,000/26.5 ≈$15,094.34; 22% isolated federal tax ≈$3,320.75 before other return items.","D: loss-first $78,000 vs gain-first $84,000 with intermediate withdrawal; no withdrawal both $91,000.","The advisory plan keeps home and emergency funds separate from retirement equity, tests bad returns and longevity, verifies beneficiaries, and never promises a safe withdrawal guarantee."],"rubric":["Precise sources and 2026 contribution eligibility (15%)","Workplace plan match, vesting and liquidity (15%)","Equal-cost retirement-tax mathematics and account rules (20%)","Social Security benefit and income-tax cash-flow analysis (20%)","Retirement decumulation, RMDs and sequence risk (20%)","Ethical fiduciary-style written recommendations and uncertainty (10%)"],"sources":["https://www.irs.gov/newsroom/401k-limit-increases-to-24500-for-2026-ira-limit-increases-to-7500","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p590b","https://www.irs.gov/instructions/i8606","https://www.ssa.gov/cola/factsheets/2026.html","https://www.ssa.gov/benefits/retirement/planner/1960-delay.html","https://portal.ct.gov/drs/individuals/connecticut-tax-tips-for-senior-citizens"]}};
